Third-party binaries redistributed with this project
===================================================

`aobs` itself is licensed under `LICENSE`. This file is about the binaries the **input archive**
carries, because those are what a release redistributes — and about the **source archive** published
beside them, because that is how the obligation those binaries carry is met.

What is redistributed
---------------------

`aobs-inputs-<release>.tar`, published as a release asset, carries:

- the **appliance apk closure** and the **toolchain apk closure** — Alpine Linux `.apk` packages,
  **unmodified**, exactly as Alpine published them, with their upstream signatures intact;
- `alpine-minirootfs-<version>-x86_64.tar.gz`, Alpine's own release tarball;
- `linux-<version>.tar.xz`, the vanilla kernel.org source tarball.

Alpine's `APKINDEX.tar.gz` is deliberately **not** among them. Alpine re-signs it whenever anything
lands in the branch, so archiving it made the archive differ between two fetches of the same package
set; `build/mkiso.sh` generates its own from the `.apk` files above and installs with
`--allow-untrusted` (#68).

`aobs-sources-<release>.tar` is published beside it and carries the corresponding source for
everything copyleft-touched in the list above. It is **not a build input** — nothing in it is read by
any build — which is why it is a separate asset with a separate list. See *Corresponding source*
below.

That the packages are unmodified is checkable rather than promised, and the check is not `apk`'s: it
is `build/inputs.sha256`, a list in this project's git history that pins every archived byte and is
enforced on hash **and on set equality** before the build reads any of them. Each `.apk` also still
carries the upstream Alpine signature it was published with, which anyone can verify against
Alpine's own keys.

Licences
--------

**The per-package licence, origin aport and aports git commit are recorded in the `NOTICE` file
inside the archive itself**, generated by `build/apkindex.py` from each package's own `.PKGINFO` —
the same file Alpine's index is generated from — so the record travels with the bytes rather than
living only in a file a redistributor may not have kept.

48 of the 114 origin aports declare a copyleft term. **9 of them, covering 18 `.apk` files, are
GPL-2.0-*only*** — `busybox`, `git`, `linux-headers`, `util-linux`, `lddtree`, `pax-utils`,
`mkinitfs`, `alpine-baselayout` and `apk-tools`. That distinction is the one that decides this file:
a package under a GPL-2.0-**or-later** or GPL-3.0 term may be taken under GPLv3, whose §6(d) permits
offering the source from a third party's server. GPLv2 §3 permits no such thing.

The manifest's `aports-commit:` field names one commit as a pointer — the aports recipe commit of
`alpine-release`, the branch's release-marker package. The complete per-package record is the
archive's own `NOTICE`.

Corresponding source
--------------------

**The corresponding source is published as a second release asset,
`aobs-sources-<release>.tar`**, and its `sha256` is bound by the signed manifest's
`sources-list-sha256` field to `build/sources.sha256` as committed at the tag. It is an
**accompaniment** under GPLv2 §3(a), not an offer and not a pointer.

For every origin aport whose declared licence names a GPL, LGPL, AGPL, MPL-2, EPL or CDDL term, it
carries both halves of what "corresponding source" means:

- the `APKBUILD` and **every patch, config and init script** the recipe names, fetched file by file
  from <https://gitlab.alpinelinux.org/alpine/aports> at the exact commit that package's `.PKGINFO`
  records — not at a branch tip, and not as a server-generated directory archive whose bytes are
  nobody's to promise;
- the **upstream release tarballs** that recipe names, fetched from Alpine's distfiles store and
  each one verified against the `sha512sums` in that same pinned `APKBUILD`.

48 origins, 306 files, ~456 MB. `build/fetch-sources.sh` is the whole of it, and
`build/sources.sha256` is the list.

For a package whose declared licence names none of those terms, no source is archived; the recorded
recipe commit is the record.

Why the sources are carried rather than pointed at
--------------------------------------------------

An earlier version of this file said buildable sources were "explicitly not promised", on the stated
premise that Alpine's distfiles retention was "unverified and plausibly as lossy as its `.apk`
retention". **That premise was measured and it was false.** The distfiles store is partitioned per
release branch, accumulates superseded versions rather than replacing them, and reaches back to
v3.10 (2019); all 123 upstream tarballs this project's origins name were fetchable from it, and all
114 recipe commits resolved. The mechanism worked better than the claim about it.

Three findings moved the posture anyway:

- **GPLv2 §3 offers three routes and this project could take none of them by pointing.** §3(a) is
  accompaniment by source. §3(b) is a written offer valid three years — which requires keeping the
  source regardless, and being the party who is asked. §3(c) passes along an offer *received*, and
  these `.apk` files were fetched from a CDN with no offer attached. GPLv3 §6(d)'s third-party-server
  allowance has no counterpart in GPLv2, and 18 of the redistributed files have no "or later" escape
  into GPLv3.
- **Even under §6(d) the duty stays here** — "you remain obligated to ensure that it is available".
  Alpine's distfiles is a single nginx host: no CDN, and 0 of 106 official Alpine mirrors carry it.
  It grew monotonically for eleven years and then had eight branch directories removed at once,
  between 2025-09-06 and 2026-01-20, on no published policy. A duty discharged through infrastructure
  this project does not control is a claim about somebody else's future conduct.
- **Upstream is already lossy today.** `ncurses-6.6-20260516.tgz` returns 404 at the URL its own
  `APKBUILD` names and survives only on distfiles. Any wording implying a rebuilder can fetch from
  upstream is false on the day of release, not in 2030.

So the sources are fetched while they are all still there, verified against hashes from a pinned git
history, and published as bytes this project controls and signs. That also lets a rebuilder in 2030
*rebuild* rather than only re-verify — a side effect, not the reason.

**What this claim does not cover.** The scope is Alpine's own declared `L:` field for each package,
not an audit of the packages' `COPYING` files; a package Alpine mislabels as permissive has no source
archived here. No lawyer has reviewed this file. Both are stated so a reader can check the claim
rather than take it.

Vendored source
---------------

Two libraries are vendored into the application tree as source, not as binaries, and carry their own
licences beside them:

- `aobs/core/vendor/embit/` — see `aobs/core/vendor/embit/LICENSE`
- `aobs/core/vendor/ur2/` — see `aobs/core/vendor/ur2/LICENSE`

`aobs/core/vendor/README.md` records which upstream commit each was taken from and why they are
vendored rather than installed. No binary artifact is present in either tree, and
`build/verify.py`'s `check_vendored_tree` fails the build if one appears.
